--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/adr/0049-cancel-all-cancels-again-an-order-pending-cancel.md
@@ -0,0 +1,66 @@
+---
+status: accepted
+date: 2026-11-11
+decision-makers: master orchestrator (Claude), on a defect the owner's coordinator confirmed on 2026-11-11
+review-by: 2026-12-11
+---
+
+# Cancel-all cancels again an order already pending cancel
+
+## Context and Problem Statement
+
+ADR 0036 has the Alpaca execution client's cancel-all skip an order the node holds as `PENDING_CANCEL`, as
+Coinbase's adapter does, "since a second cancel risks a rejection". NautilusTrader puts an order in
+`PENDING_CANCEL` when the cancel command is sent, before Alpaca answers. A cancel that gets no answer, a
+read timeout for one (ADR 0039), leaves the order `PENDING_CANCEL` while it may still work at Alpaca, and
+the skip then stops any later cancel-all from cancelling it. A confirmed defect showed exactly that
+(`test_cancel_all_cancels_again_an_order_whose_cancel_had_no_answer`). Should cancel-all skip such an
+order?
+
+## Decision Drivers
+
+* A cancel-all must not leave working an order it names, since nothing else would send its cancel again.
+* The risk ADR 0036 named, a rejection of the second cancel, has to stay without effect on the node.
+
+## Considered Options
+
+1. Keep the skip (ADR 0036).
+2. Cancel every open or in-flight order the command names, one pending cancel included.
+3. Ask Alpaca for each pending-cancel order first and cancel it only if Alpaca still shows it working.
+
+## Decision Outcome
+
+Chosen: **option 2**. The client already handles the rejection ADR 0036 feared: "Delete Order by ID"
+answers 422 when "the order is no longer cancelable", and before a 422 becomes `OrderCancelRejected` the
+client reads the order and generates nothing when Alpaca shows it canceled or pending cancel (ADR 0036,
+the retry case of ADR 0030). So a second cancel of an order Alpaca already took ends with no event, and
+one Alpaca never got cancels it. Option 3 costs a lookup per pending order and races the cancel it would
+precede; the 422 path already makes that lookup when it is needed. The test brief that confirmed the
+defect states Kraken spot, Binance spot, Bybit and Interactive Brokers include every open order in their
+cancel-all (`UNVERIFIED` this session: their sources were not re-read).
+
+### Consequences
+
+* Good: an order whose cancel got no answer is cancelled by the next cancel-all.
+* Bad: one more HTTP call, and on a 422 one lookup, for each order whose cancel is in fact in flight.
+
+### Confirmation
+
+`packages/canon-alpaca/tests/test_execution_client.py::test_cancel_all_cancels_again_an_order_whose_cancel_had_no_answer`:
+a cancel answered by a transport error, then a cancel-all, sends the cancel twice and ends `OrderCanceled`.
+A 422 for an order Alpaca shows pending cancel generating no event is
+`test_refused_repeat_of_a_cancel_alpaca_took_is_not_a_rejection` in the same file, for a single cancel;
+the two together, a cancel-all repeating a cancel Alpaca had taken, have no test of their own.
+
+## Evidence
+
+Read 2026-11-11; no network was used for this record.
+
+* `docs/adr/0036-alpaca-adapter-order-management.md`: the skip and its reason, the 422 exception for an
+  order Alpaca shows canceled or pending cancel (read in this checkout).
+* `packages/canon-alpaca/src/canon_alpaca/execution.py`: `_cancel`, `_cancel_refused` and the constant
+  `NOT_CANCELABLE`, quoting https://docs.alpaca.markets/us/reference/deleteorderbyorderid-1.md (read in
+  this checkout; the Alpaca page itself `UNVERIFIED` this session, fetched for ADR 0036 on 2026-11-10).
+* Kraken spot, Binance spot, Bybit and Interactive Brokers cancel-all including pending-cancel orders:
+  stated in the defect's test, `UNVERIFIED` this session.
+* Supersedes the `PENDING_CANCEL` skip in ADR 0036's cancel-all only; the rest of ADR 0036 stands.
